    <Section id="main-section" title="X-ray Diffraction and Microscopy">
        <p>
            Previously in this class we learned about the different types of crystal structures: Simple Cubic, BCC, and FCC.
            Now we need to figure out how we determine the structure of a given crystal. 
        </p>
    </Section>

    <SubSection id="XRD" title="XRD">
        <p>
            In physics there is something called the two-slit experiment, an illustration of this can be seen to the left. 
            In the experiment there is an incoming light with some wavelength (<InlineEquation equation="\\lambda" />).
            The light travels through 2 slits at a distance d apart from each other, diffracts and creates constructive interference. 
            This experiment gives us Bragg's Law where:
        </p>
        <p>
       \[
            n$\lambda$ = 2dSin($\theta$)
        \]        
        </p>
    </SubSection>








